        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 865,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 751,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Computes the log of the multivariate gamma function of dimension p for every element of a tensor.\n\nIt is the normalising constant of the Wishart distribution - the distribution of random covariance matrices - so it appears when you work with uncertainty about a whole matrix rather than a single number.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nIt is a sum of p ordinary log gammas, at x, x - 0.5, x - 1 and so on down to x - (p - 1)/2, plus a constant p(p - 1)/4 times log pi. So p = 1 is exactly t.special.gammaln, and each increase in p adds another rising log gamma curve: at x = 10 it is 12.8 for p = 1, 25.1 for p = 2, 36.8 for p = 3, 58.9 for p = 5.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.multigammaln\n\nThere is no argument: p starts at 1 and is set on the node.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it triggers the node. Each element is worked on separately.\n\np (int, also an inlet):\nThe dimension. Default 1, minimum 1. Changing it does not send by itself;\nthe next tensor uses it.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape as the input.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\nEvery element must be greater than (p - 1) / 2. Values at or below that come out as infinity. The demo starts at 2.6 so that p up to 5 is safe.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second; it makes 200 values from 2.6 to 10, drawn in the upper plot. The lower plot is multigammaln of each for dimension p, from 0 (bottom) to 60 (top). Drag p on the node and the curve climbs.\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.gammaln is the one-dimensional case. t.special.digamma and t.special.polygamma are its derivatives.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 735,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Computes the log of the multivariate gamma function of dimension p for every element of a tensor.\n\nIt is the normalising constant of the Wishart distribution - the distribution of random covariance matrices - so it appears when you work with uncertainty about a whole matrix rather than a single number.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nIt is a sum of p ordinary log gammas, at x, x - 0.5, x - 1 and so on down to x - (p - 1)/2, plus a constant p(p - 1)/4 times log pi. So p = 1 is exactly t.special.gammaln, and each increase in p adds another rising log gamma curve: at x = 10 it is 12.8 for p = 1, 25.1 for p = 2, 36.8 for p = 3, 58.9 for p = 5.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.multigammaln\n\nThere is no argument: p starts at 1 and is set on the node.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it triggers the node. Each element is worked on separately.\n\np (int, also an inlet):\nThe dimension. Default 1, minimum 1. Changing it does not send by itself;\nthe next tensor uses it.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape as the input.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\nEvery element must be greater than (p - 1) / 2. Values at or below that come out as infinity. The demo starts at 2.6 so that p up to 5 is safe.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second; it makes 200 values from 2.6 to 10, drawn in the upper plot. The lower plot is multigammaln of each for dimension p, from 0 (bottom) to 60 (top). Drag p on the node and the curve climbs.\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.gammaln is the one-dimensional case. t.special.digamma and t.special.polygamma are its derivatives."
        },
